/T (The lectures compare passive, assertive and aggressive people based on their body language, listening skills, expression of feelings and the outcomes of their behaviour. Assertiveness means expressing your opinions, feelings and rights confidently without violating anyone else's rights. It helps people communicate clearly, maintain healthy relationships and reach win-win situations. Practical steps include checking your relationships, dropping self- debilitating thoughts, avoiding discouraging people, following Stanley and Nancy's commandments and learning four ways to say no. Learning to say no politely is important because it helps us set boundaries without hurting others.)
/E (The lectures compare passive, assertive and aggressive people based on their body language, listening skills, expression of feelings and the outcomes of their behaviour. Assertiveness means expressing your opinions, feelings and rights confidently without violating anyone else's rights. It helps people communicate clearly, maintain healthy relationships and reach win-win situations. Practical steps include checking your relationships, dropping self- debilitating thoughts, avoiding discouraging people, following Stanley and Nancy's commandments and learning four ways to say no. Learning to say no politely is important because it helps us set boundaries without hurting others.)
