/Title (The lectures discuss forty traits that make others dislike us and their opposites that make people like us. Examples include egotism, aggression, pessimism, fault-finding, gossiping, false promises, moodiness and favouritism, set against selflessness, optimism, giving credit, politeness, trustworthiness, honesty and humility. These qualities influence how people perceive us and how successfully we build relationships. Developing good people skills requires understanding others, respecting their opinions and treating everyone fairly.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 3276 0 R
/Next 3307 0 R
>>
endobj
3307 0 obj
<<
/Title (Being a good listener and avoiding negative conversations can also help create a more respectful environment. Attractiveness has physical, mental, emotional and spiritual layers. Physically, it means neat footwear, a fit body, good diet and sleep, and a signature personality. Mentally, it means intellectual charm and a touch of mystery. Emotionally, it means remembering names, valuing yourself and being yourself. Spiritually, it comes down to mindfulness, deeper connection, helping without expectation and "paying it forward." The main lesson is that attractiveness is not limited to physical appearance; our behaviour, attitude, confidence and kindness also influence how others see us.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 3276 0 R
/Prev 3306 0 R
>>
endobj
